<section class="three-tool-section" id="invest-shares"><h3>How to calculate Zakat on stocks, shares and ETFs</h3>
<p>Intent and the form of ownership matter. <strong>Shares bought to trade for resale</strong> are often treated much like inventory: their current market value is assessed. For <strong>long-term investment shares</strong>, scholars may seek your proportionate ownership of the company's cash, trade stock and qualifying receivables rather than charging a flat amount on machinery, offices or other fixed assets. ETF and fund structures can require looking through multiple holdings.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/zakat/zakat-and-investment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK investment guide, reviewed in February 2026</a> discusses both methods and an optional <strong>25% proxy</strong> when genuine asset data is not accessible. The proxy is <em>not</em> a Quranic fixed percentage or mandatory global rule. You must deliberately select it only if your qualified guidance accepts it.</p>
<p>Dividends or cash already sitting in an exchange or brokerage account should not be counted twice if you included them under general savings. Whether a pension or a restricted investment account should be included depends on access, beneficial ownership and applicable jurisprudence.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="invest-crypto"><h3>How to assess cryptocurrency, stablecoins and utility tokens</h3>
<p>Digital coins commonly used as a means of exchange may be assessed in a cash-like way under some contemporary approaches. Assets intentionally acquired for trade or resale can also be assessed at market value. <strong>Utility tokens, NFTs, governance rights, liquidity-pool positions, locked balances and DeFi holdings</strong> can require different analysis of the underlying rights and ownership.</p>
<p>Do not assume a token is either automatically Zakat-free or always fully Zakatable. Our calculator asks you to classify it and, for a utility/other token, enter a separately established qualifying percentage. See <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/zakat/zakat-and-cryptocurrency/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK's crypto discussion (2026)</a>. If unsure, review your asset with a scholar familiar with digital instruments rather than using a software guess.</p>
<p>The app does not access private keys, connect wallets, automatically convert foreign currencies or download market quotes. Values entered should be from a credible exchange or pricing source for your relevant assessment date and consistent currency.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="invest-method"><h3>Step-by-step stocks and crypto Zakat calculation</h3>
<ol class="three-tool-steps"><li>Choose your Zakat review date and check the correct Nisab and hawl position with your school.</li>
<li>List each share, fund or crypto holding once, with a verified current value in one currency.</li>
<li>Mark trading/resale shares at full value only if that description matches your intention and guidance.</li>
<li>For long-term shares, enter a supported underlying qualifying-asset percentage or deliberately choose the 25% proxy if authorised for you.</li>
<li>For cryptocurrency, distinguish monetary/trading assets from utility rights. Get independent advice before setting the percentage of more unusual holdings.</li>
<li>Add other qualifying assets that are not already included, deduct only permitted liabilities, and enter a verified applicable Nisab from the <a href="/tools/nisab-calculator/">Nisab Calculator</a>.</li>
<li>Review the conditional 2.5% estimate; it is not due solely because an app produced a number.</li></ol>
</section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="invest-example"><h3>Worked example: trading shares, long-term shares and crypto</h3>
<div class="three-tool-example"><p><strong>Invented values only:</strong> Assume £3,000 of shares assessed at full trading value, £8,000 of long-term shares using an expressly accepted 25% proxy (£2,000 assessed), and £1,000 of coin holdings independently classified as fully zakatable. Add £500 in other qualifying wealth and subtract £500 in permitted debt. The assessed net wealth is <strong>£3,000 + £2,000 + £1,000 + £500 − £500 = £6,000</strong>.</p><p>With a purely hypothetical, externally selected £2,000 Nisab and a satisfied hawl, the arithmetic is <strong>£6,000 × 2.5% = £150</strong>. This £2,000 figure is <strong>not</strong> the current UK Nisab, the 25% proxy is not universal, and neither the amount nor the classifications are religiously certified.</p></div>
<p>If the underlying long-term company's zakatable asset proportion is instead 10%, that £8,000 holding contributes £800, not £2,000. The total and conditional result change accordingly. That is why classifications and source information matter more than a generic calculator formula.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="invest-nisab"><h3>Nisab, the lunar year and investment eligibility</h3>
<p>Nisab is a gold- or silver-based minimum assessed under the school you follow. Hawl is a lunar-year condition, generally about 354–355 days, but the details of new purchases and changing holdings can vary. Our calculator will show an <em>arithmetic-only</em> 2.5% figure without a user-entered Nisab, but it will <strong>not label that figure payable</strong>.</p>
<p>For timing use the <a href="/tools/zakat-anniversary-calculator/">Zakat Anniversary Calculator</a>, and for the full set of cash, gold, investments and business debts see the <a href="/tools/zakat-calculator/">Zakat Calculator</a>. The investment estimator is designed for an isolated source-backed breakdown, not for assuming that the whole contents of a trading platform are Zakatable or Sharia-compliant.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="invest-pitfalls"><h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>
<div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>Double-counting cash</h4><p>Do not add the same exchange balance under both investments and savings.</p></div><div><h4>Wrong stock treatment</h4><p>Resale and long-term ownership can have different asset bases.</p></div><div><h4>Blanket crypto exclusions</h4><p>Unusual tokens need individual ownership and asset-rights review.</p></div><div><h4>Invented Nisab or prices</h4><p>Use verified current figures and the correct assessment date; no rate is fetched here.</p></div></div>
</section>
